-## Example
+`Observation.device` identifies recording hardware. Supply `gatewayDevice` only when an
+application or device actually mediated the exchange. The application that saved the
+HealthKit object and the converter are separate Provenance roles. HealthKit does not
+discriminate application sources from direct devices, so Grove emits no author by
+default. Pass `sourceActor` only after establishing the source kind. Grove then derives
+the author name, identifier, and version from that sample's `HKSourceRevision`; callers
+cannot substitute another identity. Application authors use the Apple bundle identifier
+and, when present, the source's typed version string. Supported Bluetooth Low Energy
+device authors use the dedicated source-device namespace only when the HealthKit-provided
+linkable identifier is authorized for exchange. Grove's own converter always requires a
+typed software version because it controls that build provenance. A user-entered flag
+establishes `manual-entry`, but does not identify a Patient performer.
+
+Pass governing ResearchStudy references through `researchStudies`. Grove writes the
+standard `workflow-researchStudy` extension; the study identifies its versioned protocol
+with `ResearchStudy.protocol` pointing to a PlanDefinition. The converter does not infer
+study membership or treat a study definition as source data.
+
+HealthKit dictionary metadata is not copied generically. Only mappings published by the
+adapter guide are retained; unsupported keys are omitted until their FHIR semantics are
+defined and tested.
+
+Clinical records already encoded as FHIR R4 can still be decoded through
+``HealthKit/HKClinicalRecord/resource()``. They do not pass through the Observation
+adapter contract described above.
 
